# {{.Name}}

A command-line tool built on [rungrad](https://github.com/vincentsch/rungrad),
so it speaks the agent-ready CLI spec: stable `--json` output, `--dry-run`
previews, destructive confirmation, deterministic results, self-describing help,
and an offline-checkable self-update.

## Build and run

```bash
go mod tidy
go build -o {{.Name}} .
./{{.Name}} widget list
./{{.Name}} widget list --json
./{{.Name}} widget create gamma --dry-run
./{{.Name}} widget delete alpha --dry-run
./{{.Name}} update --check
```

If you want to develop against a local rungrad checkout instead of the tagged
module version, add a local replace:

```bash
go mod edit -replace github.com/vincentsch/rungrad=/path/to/rungrad
go mod tidy
```

## Score it against the spec

```bash
rungrad score ./{{.Name}} \
  --read        "widget list" \
  --mutate      "widget create demo" \
  --destructive "widget delete alpha" \
  --update
```

The starter implements read output, a mutating dry-run, a safe destructive
command, and an offline update, so those rules score. It intentionally omits the
auth, name-resolution, missing-resource, backend-error, forbidden, rate-limit, and
credential-secret fixtures, which the scorer reports as not-applicable rather than
failures.

> The generated `update` command uses an offline fixed fetcher so the project can
> be scored locally without network access. Replace it with `update.GitHubFetcher`
> (or equivalent release wiring) before distributing real releases.
